This keeps the stream idea but applies it to the full array template. Each stack record represents one price and all the consecutive days grouped under it. A new price joins every record on top that is less than or equal to it.

An increasing input such as `[10, 20, 30]` produces `[1, 2, 3]`. A decreasing input such as `[30, 20, 10]` produces `[1, 1, 1]`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    public int[] stockSpan(int[] arr, int n) {
        int[] result = new int[n];
        Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int currentSpan = 1;

            while (!stack.isEmpty() && stack.peek()[0] <= arr[i]) {
                currentSpan += stack.pop()[1];
            }

            stack.push(new int[] {arr[i], currentSpan});
            result[i] = currentSpan;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(n) for all days. Although one iteration may remove many records, each record is pushed and popped only once. Space complexity: O(n). The input array is not changed.

A direct scan is easy to check. For each day, move left while the earlier price is less than or equal to the current price. The count includes the current day, so the initial span is 1.

This is the slower version, but it is useful when the input is small or when keeping the logic very clear matters.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution
{
public:
    vector<int> stockSpan(vector<int> arr, int n) {
        vector<int> result(n, 1);

        for (int day = 0; day < n; ++day) {
            int previous = day - 1;

            while (previous >= 0 && arr[previous] <= arr[day]) {
                ++result[day];
                --previous;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(n^2) in the worst case. Space complexity: O(n) for the result array. The input array is not changed.

The span can skip groups of days that were already counted. If `arr[j]` is not greater than today's price, add `span[j]` and jump back by that many days. This avoids checking every price separately in many cases.

For `[100, 80, 60, 70]`, the span for 70 starts at 1, adds the span of 60, and becomes 2. The price 80 then stops the search.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] stockSpan(int[] arr, int n) {
        int[] span = new int[n];

        for (int day = 0; day < n; day++) {
            span[day] = 1;
            int previous = day - 1;

            while (previous >= 0 && arr[previous] <= arr[day]) {
                span[day] += span[previous];
                previous -= span[previous];
            }
        }

        return span;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: O(n) overall because the saved spans let the search jump over known groups. Space complexity: O(n) for the output and saved spans. The input array stays unchanged.

An empty array returns an empty result, and a decreasing array gives span 1 for every day.

Here the stack keeps indices instead of storing spans with each price. After removing smaller or equal prices, the index at the top is the nearest previous greater price. The distance from that index gives the answer.

The first day has no previous greater price, so its span is 1. The same rule also handles a new price that is greater than every price seen before.

```cpp
#include <stack>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution
{
public:
    vector<int> stockSpan(vector<int> arr, int n) {
        vector<int> result(n);
        stack<int> indices;

        for (int day = 0; day < n; ++day) {
            while (!indices.empty() && arr[indices.top()] <= arr[day]) {
                indices.pop();
            }

            if (indices.empty()) {
                result[day] = day + 1;
            } else {
                result[day] = day - indices.top();
            }

            indices.push(day);
        }

        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: O(n), since each index is pushed once and popped once. Space complexity: O(n) for the stack and result. The input array is only read.

For equal prices, the `<=` check is important because equal prices belong to the current span.

## Intuition
We can use pse/nge approach and also track the span for each index and continue building upon that span for the next indices.

## Approach
This code uses a Monotonic Stack to efficiently calculate the stock span for each day. Instead of looking back one by one, it jumps back using previously calculated answers.

Here is the step-by-step logic:

1. Track Indices: A stack (st) is used to store the indices of the stock prices, not the prices themselves.

2. Initialize Span: For every new day i, its default span is 1 (counting itself).

3. Look Back and Accumulate: * It checks the stack. If the previous price (at the index on top of the stack) is less than or equal to the current price arr[i], that previous day and all days it already absorbed are part of the current day's span.

    -  It pops that previous index from the stack and adds its previously calculated span (ans[st.pop()]) to the current day's span.

    -  It repeats this until it hits a day with a higher price or the stack is empty.

4. Save and Push: It saves the total span for the current day into ans[i] and then pushes the current index i onto the stack for future days to compare against.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int[] stockSpan(int[] arr, int n) {
        int[] ans = new int[n];

        Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++){

            int currAns = 1;

            while(!st.isEmpty() && arr[st.peek()] <= arr[i]){
                currAns += ans[st.pop()];
            }
            ans[i] = currAns;

            st.push(i);
        }

        return ans;
    }
}

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
Even though there is a while loop inside a for loop, the time complexity is strictly linear. Every index from 0 to n-1 is pushed onto the stack exactly once and popped from the stack at most once. Since stack push() and pop() operations take $O(1)$ time, the total operations across all iterations sum up to $O(N)$

**Space Complexity:**
The Stack can hold up to $N$ elements in the worst-case scenario (e.g., if the given array is sorted in strictly decreasing order, nothing will ever get popped, and all indices will be pushed onto the stack). Total auxiliary space is $O(N)$.

Intuition

For every day, we need to find how many consecutive previous days (including the current day) have stock prices less than or equal to today’s price.

A brute force approach would check all previous elements for every index, resulting in O(n
2
