To optimize this, we use a monotonic stack that stores indices of elements in decreasing order of stock prices.

If the current price is greater than or equal to the stack top price, those elements cannot affect future answers, so we pop them.
After popping:
if the stack becomes empty, it means all previous elements are smaller, so span = i + 1
otherwise, the nearest greater element exists at st.top(), so span = i - st.top()

This allows us to process every element only once.

Approach
Create an answer array initialized with 1.
Use a stack to store indices of stock prices.
Traverse the array:
Pop all indices whose value is less than or equal to the current value.
If stack becomes empty:
span = i + 1
Else:
span = i - st.top()
Push current index into the stack.
Return the answer array.

The stack always maintains indices of elements in strictly decreasing order.

